Empathic Embodiment Transfer

Unlocking Internet-Scale Experience for Embodied Intelligence Through Self–Other Transduction

Status: Concept Whitepaper / Architectural Seed
Version: 0.1
Date: August 2026

⸻

Abstract

Robotics is commonly described as suffering from a fundamental data disadvantage relative to language and vision models. Large language models inherit trillions of tokens generated by humans; robots appear to require expensive, embodiment-specific interaction data gathered through teleoperation, simulation, controlled demonstrations, or physical deployment.

This paper argues that a substantial portion of that disadvantage is not an absence of data but an interface mismatch between existing experience and robotic embodiment.

The internet already contains an enormous corpus of embodied behavior: humans manipulating objects, animals navigating environments, workers using tools, athletes intercepting moving targets, children exploring unfamiliar objects, vehicles moving through traffic, instructional videos demonstrating procedures, games recording state-action-outcome sequences, and fictional media depicting social and physical interactions.

Most of this information is presently categorized as video, media, or human behavior rather than robot training data.

The proposed architecture, Empathic Embodiment Transfer (EET), attempts to change that categorization.

Instead of requiring a robot to directly imitate another body, EET trains an embodied agent to construct:

1. a model of self,
2. a model of other agents,
3. an embodiment-independent representation of goals, constraints, affordances, transformations, and actions,
4. a translation mechanism from observed behavior into that representation,
5. and a second translation mechanism from the representation into actions available to its own body.

The key transformation is:

\text{OTHER EXPERIENCE}
\rightarrow
\text{EMBODIMENT-INVARIANT EXPERIENCE}
\rightarrow
\text{SELF-EXECUTABLE EXPERIENCE}

In this framework, computational analogues of anthropomorphism, personification, perspective-taking, and empathy are not merely social behaviors. They become mechanisms for data transduction.

A robot need not phenomenologically experience empathy. It needs the functional ability to ask:

What state was that agent in, what was it trying to change, what constraints governed the interaction, and how would my embodiment accomplish an equivalent transformation?

If this translation can be learned reliably, robot-specific experience becomes increasingly valuable as calibration data rather than the sole source of intelligence.

The resulting thesis is simple:

Do not give every robot a separate childhood. Teach robots how to inherit experience acquired by other bodies.

