1. The Data Scarcity Assumption

Modern AI benefited from a historical accident: civilization had already generated enormous quantities of machine-readable language, imagery, audio, software, and video before foundation-model training began.

Robotics appears different.

Physical action datasets are expensive because they commonly require:

* physical hardware,
* teleoperators,
* instrumentation,
* controlled environments,
* specialized sensors,
* annotation,
* resets after failures,
* hardware maintenance,
* safety supervision,
* and repeated demonstrations across different robot morphologies.

Consequently, robot data is frequently treated as intrinsically scarce.

This has generated a straightforward scaling strategy:

\text{more robot data}
\rightarrow
\text{better robot}

That strategy is valid, but incomplete.

A second route exists:

\text{better translation}
\rightarrow
\text{more existing data becomes robot data}

The distinction is economically important.

If an intelligent system cannot interpret human experience because a person possesses five fingers while a gripper possesses two jaws, the missing resource may not be another million gripper demonstrations.

The missing resource may be a sufficiently good cross-embodiment compiler.

Research is already demonstrating portions of this possibility. Recent surveys identify robot learning from human video as a major route around robot-data scarcity, while methods such as latent-action pretraining learn behavior-relevant structure from video without conventional robot action labels. (arXiv⁠￼)

