2. The Category Error

Consider a video of a person opening a drawer.

At the surface level, the recording contains:

* a human arm,
* a human hand,
* fingers,
* a drawer,
* a handle,
* a trajectory.

A naïve imitation system may attempt to reproduce those human motions.

But the deeper event is something like:

\text{detect movable object}
\rightarrow
\text{identify interaction point}
\rightarrow
\text{establish contact}
\rightarrow
\text{apply constrained force}
\rightarrow
\text{translate object}
\rightarrow
\text{reach desired state}

The second representation is much more portable.

The relevant invariant was never five fingers.

The invariant was approximately:

establish controllable contact with a constrained object and produce displacement along its permitted degree of freedom.

A human hand, industrial gripper, hook, suction mechanism, robotic claw, or sufficiently unusual future manipulator could instantiate that same operator differently.

This produces the central distinction:

\boxed{\text{Observed motion} \neq \text{underlying action}}

Motion belongs to an embodiment.

Action can often be represented at a deeper level.

⸻

3. From Mimicry to Empathic Transfer

Humans routinely understand actions they cannot reproduce exactly.

A person can watch:

* a bird landing,
* a dog squeezing beneath a fence,
* an excavator digging,
* a goalkeeper intercepting a ball,
* a surgeon tying a knot,
* or an octopus opening a container,

and infer something about goals, constraints, timing, causality, strategy, and affordances without possessing the observed body.

This is possible because observers do not only encode joint trajectories.

They infer latent structure.

EET proposes using the same computational principle deliberately.

The robot observes:

O_t = \text{observed state of another agent and world}

and infers:

Z =
\{
goal,
objects,
relations,
affordances,
constraints,
contact,
trajectory,
causal transformation,
risk,
termination condition
\}

It then solves:

A_{\text{self}}^*
=
\arg\max_A
P(
Z_{\text{desired}}
\mid
A,
M_{\text{self}},
W
)

where:

* M_{\text{self}} is the robot’s self-model,
* W is its current world model,
* A_{\text{self}} is an action sequence available to its embodiment.

The machine therefore does not ask:

How do I move like the human?

It asks:

What did the human accomplish, and what is the equivalent operation for me?

That is the transition from imitation to transduction.

⸻

4. The Self-Model Is the Translation Anchor

Cross-embodiment transfer requires a reference frame.

That reference frame is the robot’s self-model.

A practical self-model does not require philosophical claims about machine consciousness or subjective experience.

It can be defined operationally as the system’s continuously updated model of:

M_{\text{self}} =
\{
body,
sensors,
effectors,
joint\ limits,
reachability,
strength,
geometry,
latency,
proprioception,
viewpoint,
skills,
damage/state,
uncertainty
\}

The robot learns:

This sensory change occurred because I moved.

These pixels belong to my manipulator.

These actions are reachable by me.

This force causes my body to move this way.

That object moved because I contacted it.

Self-recognition therefore becomes less about identifying a visual reflection and more about learning agency boundaries.

The question is:

\text{What portion of observed world-state transition is attributable to my action?}

This gives the system a computational distinction between:

SELF
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
WORLD

Once that distinction exists, another agent can be modeled analogically:

OTHER
=
\text{another bounded causal controller operating through another embodiment}

This creates the bridge required for self–other translation.

