7. Empathy as a Compiler

The central EET operator is functional empathy:

Represent another agent’s situation well enough to determine what equivalent action would make sense from the observer’s own embodiment.

This can be expressed as:

T_{\text{other}\rightarrow\text{self}}
=
D_{\text{self}}
\circ
I
\circ
E_{\text{other}}

where:

* E_{\text{other}} encodes observed behavior,
* I produces an embodiment-invariant intermediate representation,
* D_{\text{self}} compiles that representation into executable self-actions.

Thus:

\boxed{
\text{Observation}
\rightarrow
\text{Other-model}
\rightarrow
\text{Invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{Self-model}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
}

This intermediate representation is the architectural heart of the proposal.

It functions similarly to an intermediate representation in a compiler.

Different source languages can be translated into a shared IR.

Different hardware targets can then compile the same IR into different machine instructions.

For robotics:

Human

Animal

Robot_A

Game\ Avatar

all compile toward:

\boxed{\text{Embodied Action IR}}

which can then compile outward toward:

Robot_B

