11. The Internet Becomes an Embodied Experience Corpus

Once the intermediate representation is sufficiently general, many media categories acquire new training value.

11.1 Instructional Video

Examples:

* cooking,
* carpentry,
* automotive repair,
* cleaning,
* assembly,
* surgery,
* crafts,
* machine operation.

These sources contain comparatively explicit relationships between intention and action.

⸻

11.2 Everyday Human Activity

Examples:

* opening doors,
* carrying objects,
* navigating rooms,
* packing,
* retrieving items,
* manipulating tools,
* avoiding collisions.

The information density of an individual clip may be low, but the corpus is immense.

⸻

11.3 Animal Behavior

Animal video provides embodiments dramatically different from conventional robots.

This is valuable precisely because it forces the model away from superficial mimicry.

Potential transferable structures include:

* route planning,
* obstacle negotiation,
* pursuit and evasion,
* balance,
* exploration,
* object inspection,
* cooperative behavior,
* recovery after failure.

The objective is not:

move like a cat.

It is:

determine which behavioral invariants survive the transition from cat → robot.

⸻

11.4 Sports

Sports footage contains enormous quantities of:

* trajectory prediction,
* interception,
* timing,
* collision avoidance,
* body coordination,
* opponent modeling,
* team coordination,
* momentum management,
* recovery behavior.

A goalkeeper does not merely teach humanoid goalkeeping.

The sequence:

observe\ trajectory
\rightarrow
predict\ future\ intersection
\rightarrow
move\ before\ arrival

is a general interception operator.

⸻

11.5 Video Games

Games deserve special attention because many implicitly expose:

S_t
\rightarrow
A_t
\rightarrow
S_{t+1}

Games can therefore provide:

* navigation,
* exploration,
* planning,
* tool selection,
* resource allocation,
* multi-agent strategy,
* state-transition prediction,
* recovery from failure.

Their principal limitation is a domain gap between simulated and real physics.

Consequently, game-derived experience should primarily teach strategy and causal abstractions, unless physical dynamics have been independently validated.

⸻

11.6 Film and Television

Fiction is both valuable and dangerous.

It contains enormous amounts of information about:

* object semantics,
* human interaction,
* intentions,
* social expectations,
* narrative causality,
* planning,
* conversation,
* environmental conventions.

But it also contains:

* edited chronology,
* impossible physics,
* staged behavior,
* cinematic shortcuts,
* implausible causality.

EET therefore requires provenance-aware learning.

A movie should not receive the same physical-confidence weight as calibrated robotic telemetry.

