13. Robot Data Does Not Disappear

EET does not imply that physical robotic experience is unnecessary.

Certain quantities are inherently embodiment-specific:

* motor torque,
* actuator latency,
* friction,
* compliance,
* backlash,
* tactile response,
* sensor noise,
* mass distribution,
* joint limitations,
* structural deformation,
* damage,
* battery behavior,
* contact dynamics.

These require direct calibration.

The proposed change is therefore:

Conventional model

\text{Robot intelligence}
\approx
\text{robot-specific experience}

EET model

\text{Robot intelligence}
\approx
\text{shared world experience}
+
\text{embodiment calibration}
+
\text{local adaptation}

Robot data becomes disproportionately valuable for learning the delta between the inherited model and this particular body.

