14. Embodiment Calibration

A newly instantiated robot can perform a structured calibration curriculum.

For example:

Reach

predict reachable workspace
attempt motion
observe actual workspace
update self-model

Contact

predict contact point
touch object
measure discrepancy
update geometry

Force

predict force → displacement
apply safe force
measure result
update dynamics

Latency

issue command
measure response delay
update temporal model

Manipulation

compile inherited ActionIR
execute
compare predicted vs observed outcome
update embodiment compiler

Thus:

prediction
\rightarrow
action
\rightarrow
observation
\rightarrow
delta
\rightarrow
model\ update

The robot’s childhood becomes a calibration phase, not a reconstruction of all accumulated human knowledge.

⸻

15. Recursive Self–Other Learning

The mechanism can improve recursively.

Initially:

OTHER \rightarrow SELF

But after executing the transferred action, the system now possesses:

SELF \rightarrow WORLD

experience.

It can compare:

\text{Predicted outcome}

against:

\text{Observed outcome}

and calculate:

\Delta =
Observed - Predicted

That delta may reveal:

* an incorrect self-model,
* an incorrect interpretation of the demonstrator,
* an incorrect physical assumption,
* an unsuitable affordance,
* an embodiment incompatibility,
* or a genuinely new environmental property.

The corrected model then improves future transfer.

The complete loop becomes:

\boxed{
observe
\rightarrow
model\ other
\rightarrow
extract\ invariant
\rightarrow
project\ into\ self
\rightarrow
simulate
\rightarrow
act
\rightarrow
measure\ delta
\rightarrow
update
}

This is not one-shot imitation.

It is recursive transduction.

⸻

16. Learning From Failure

Failed transfer is itself useful data.

Suppose:

Human:
grasps lid
twists
opens jar

Robot attempt:

grips lid
twists
object slips

The system should not merely mark:

FAIL

It should diagnose the violated invariant:

required_torque > available_contact_friction

Then search for alternate embodiments of the same goal:

increase normal force
change contact surface
brace container
use tool
change grip position

Thus the transferred lesson becomes more general after failure than before.

