17. Empathy Produces Counterfactual Training Data

Once self–other mapping works bidirectionally, observations can generate counterfactuals.

For an observed human action:

What would I have done?

For its own action:

What would a human have needed to do?

For another robot:

Which differences were caused by morphology rather than strategy?

For an animal:

Which variables mattered despite radically different anatomy?

These comparisons allow the system to estimate:

INVARIANT
=
\bigcap
\{
successful\ embodiments
\}

The more diverse the embodiments, the easier it may become to identify what truly belongs to the task rather than to a particular body.

Paradoxically, difference can improve abstraction.

