20. Economic Consequence: Translation Can Substitute for Collection

Suppose the available information reservoirs are:

D_R = robot\ data

D_H = human\ physical\ video

D_A = animal\ behavior

D_G = game/simulation\ trajectories

D_M = other\ media

A conventional robotics system may effectively use:

D_{\text{usable}}
\approx
D_R

An EET system attempts:

D_{\text{usable}}
=
D_R
+
\alpha D_H
+
\beta D_A
+
\gamma D_G
+
\delta D_M

where:

0 \leq \alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta \leq 1

represent the quality of each transduction channel.

The strategic objective therefore becomes not merely maximizing raw data collection.

It becomes maximizing:

\boxed{\text{Transduction Efficiency}}

or:

\eta_T =
\frac{\text{useful transferable experience extracted}}
{\text{available external experience}}

A sufficiently large improvement in \eta_T could unlock more effective training information than an equivalent investment in proprietary physical data collection.

