Canonical Handle

Empathic Embodiment Transfer — EET

Definition: A learning architecture in which an embodied agent uses a self-model and models of other agents to translate externally observed experience into embodiment-invariant action representations and then reinstantiate those representations through its own capabilities, using prediction error from execution to recursively improve the translation.

Compression:

OTHER → INVARIANT → SELF → ACT → Δ → UPDATE

Core claim:

Robot-data scarcity is partly a transduction problem.

Design objective:

maximize transferable experience per unit of new physical robot data

Reconstruction cue:

Empathy is the compiler.
Self is the target architecture.
Experience is portable code.
Embodiment is an implementation detail until physics proves otherwise.